## .NET 8 Migration: `TimedExecutor`

### Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET Core+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also removed**
3. The idiomatic replacement is `CancellationToken` + `Task` with cooperative cancellation

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Async-first overload (preferred)
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Usage

```csharp
var executor = new TimedExecutor();

// The work must observe the token — this is cooperative cancellation
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(ct =>
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // check periodically
        DoChunk(i);
    }
    return ComputeResult();
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Non-cooperative work won't stop** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cancellation is *cooperative*. If `work` never checks the token, it runs to completion despite the timeout. |
| **Signature change is breaking** | Callers must update from `Func<T>` → `Func<CancellationToken, T>`. For a transitional shim, wrap the old signature but know it can't actually cancel. |
| **`task.Wait()` wraps in `AggregateException`** | Use `GetAwaiter().GetResult()` to unwrap cleanly. |
| **Fire-and-forget leaks** | If you add a "walk away after timeout" wrapper that doesn't await the task, the orphaned work keeps consuming resources. |
| **`ConfigureAwait(false)`** | Add it in library code if you don't need the sync context. |

### Transitional Shim (if callers can't change yet)

```csharp
// ⚠️ Cannot actually cancel uncooperative work — it just abandons the task
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

    try
    {
        // Block with a real timeout instead of relying on token
        if (!task.Wait(timeout))
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
    catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
    {
        throw ex.InnerException!;
    }

    return task.Result;
}
```

> **Bottom line:** `Thread.Abort()` provided *preemptive* cancellation (unsafe, could corrupt state). .NET 8 requires *cooperative* cancellation via `CancellationToken`. Propagate tokens into all work—especially I/O calls, loops, and library APIs that accept them.
